                <pb facs="1225" n="1131" sameAs="frus:frus1887/pg1131"/>

                <head>No. 697.<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Bayard to Mr. Straus</hi>.</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Department of State</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1887-09-22"><hi rendition="#italic">September</hi> 22,
                        1887</date>.</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 46.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have received your No. 24, of the 6th
                    instant, concerning the proposed law for the regulation of colporteurs, and
                    desire to say that you appear to have acted in-this matter very discreetly.
                    There is no objection to your associating the British embassador with you in
                    your efforts to secure satisfactory amendments to a law which in its operations
                    affect the interests of British societies as much as our own. Your course is
                    therefore approved, and a favorable outcome awaited.</p>
                <p>I am, etc.,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">T. F. Bayard.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
